[
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"657719\">Terence Tao</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/458659-Equational/topic/Future.20directions/near/473388585\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Explore triple relations of the form \"Equation X and Equation Y implies Equation Z\". This was for instance proposed by Pace Nielsen in <a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2024/09/25/a-pilot-project-in-universal-algebra-to-explore-new-ways-to-collaborate-and-use-machine-assistance/#comment-685891\">this blog comment</a>, and I think elsewhere as well. This will be harder for several reasons, most notably because 3-SAT is way harder than 2-SAT, but the relations we obtain in this project will help reduce the problem significantly. As Pace suggests, we may have to cut down to just three operations instead of four to obtain a feasible project.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I am thinking of exploring that direction, and wondered if someone made any start/progress on that. My initial thinking was to take the existing counterexamples and derive the easier \"not implies\" relations, then target relations of the form \"Theorem X + commutativity implies Theorem Y\" for non-implicating pairs.</p>\n<p>Some of my motivation comes from AI research, so I intend to make some proofs and counterexamples by hand, then fine-tune a small LLM on the task with the goal of making it produce future work on that.</p>\n<p>Anyone here tried something along those line, or want to cooperate on this?</p>",
        "id": 597383789,
        "sender_full_name": "Reuven Peleg",
        "timestamp": 1779612722
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <a href=\"#narrow/channel/458659-Equational/topic/Dedicated.20finite.2B677.20graph/near/593118184\">https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/458659-Equational/topic/Dedicated.20finite.2B677.20graph/near/593118184</a></p>",
        "id": 597397483,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1779626756
    },
    {
